    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Дела хорошо пошли?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Business is going well?' You'll hear 'things' all the time in the show, here connected with 'let's go'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Много работы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A lot of work.' Notice the word order: 'a lot' comes first, and 'of work' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я взял племянника.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I hired my nephew.' Here 'I' sets up the idea, while 'nephew' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Его зовут Суслан.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'His name is Suslan.' Here 'him' sets up the idea, while 'Suslan' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Что ты привёз?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What did you bring?' Chef Viktor starts with 'what' and pairs it with 'you' to make the point clear. Then Timur replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Суслан, разгружай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Suslan, unload.' Timur starts with 'Suslan' and pairs it with 'unload' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "suslan",
      "t": "Я нанимался водителем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I was hired as a driver.' Listen for the word meaning 'I' right alongside 'sign up for' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "suslan",
      "t": "А не грузчиком.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Not as a loader.' In Russian, 'mover' leads into 'mover' in a very natural everyday phrasing. And Timur jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вот скотина.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What a bastard.' The first content word means 'brute', and 'brute' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вот ваша рыба, шеф.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here is your fish, Chef.' The first content word means 'fish', and 'chef' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Зачем мне рыба?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why do I need fish?' A compact expression built around 'what for' and 'fish'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Увози обратно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Take it back.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'take away' straight to 'back'. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
